Route each UDP destination of the tun inbound by its own rule, behind an
outbound tagged keq-udp-per-exit.

proxy/tun/udp_fullcone.go kept one flow per source address and dispatched it
once, with the destination of its first datagram. Every later datagram from
that socket went out through the same outbound whatever the rules said for its
address: a game whose voice socket first talked to an address outside the
user's routes kept its voice server on that exit, and a socket whose first
datagram matched direct sent everything after it past the tunnel.

With the mark in the config the flow key is the source plus the outbound the
router picks for the destination (the tun inbound tag and the original target
go into the routing context, so process rules and the uid-owner patch work as
for any connection). A socket that stays on one exit keeps one flow, so P2P
still sees one external port; an address routed elsewhere gets a flow of its
own. The router runs once per source and destination, 4096 pairs kept.

Off without the mark, and udp_fullcone.go then works as upstream: P2P traffic
that learns its address from a STUN server on one exit and reaches the peer
through another no longer gets through directly (checked on a netns lab with
pion ICE, 2026-10-17). keqdroid adds the outbound, a blackhole nothing routes
to, from Settings, core settings.

Android and desktop: since the move off keqrnel the desktop TUN is xray's own
tun inbound as well, built from the same tree by tool/build_xray_cores.ps1.

